    65	3D shell completion makes M mesh-dependent. Any **fixed-mode** panel uses one explicit, hashed mode list
    66	per (dimension, M): 2D taken at 1024², 3D at 65³, reused at every mesh (these are new configurations
    67	and need Q1/Q2). **Nominal-policy** panels keep per-mesh lists, labelled "deployment policy (mode set
    68	varies with mesh)", and report the symmetric difference against the frozen list.
    69	
    70	## 5. Compute
    71	- Tufts first (`ssh -o ConnectTimeout=20 tufts-login`, `sbatch`, `gpu` partition only, never `preempt`).
    72	  Venv `/cluster/tufts/paralab/tawal01/ae-research/venv`; `JAX_DEFAULT_MATMUL_PRECISION=highest`; f64;
    73	  GPU preflight (exit 42 if not gpu); grep `jax_backend=gpu` in every log. One job per directory under
    74	  your namespace `/cluster/tufts/paralab/tawal01/<namespace>/<job>/`. `squeue -u tawal01` before AND
    75	  after every submit. Pull results, then delete the cluster job dir.
    76	- **Campaign-wide cap: ≤ 8 concurrent cluster jobs across all lanes.** Your lane's own cap is in your
    77	  brief; never exceed it. Before each submit, also run `squeue -u tawal01 -h | wc -l` and do not submit
    78	  if it would push the account above 8.
    79	- Local GB10: only via `jaxrun`, **at most one local jaxrun per lane**, CPU preferred for analysis
    80	  (`JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu`). The GB10 GPU has corrupted results inside one process before ("Red zone",
    81	  a wrong condition number): **every GPU number comes from the cluster.** Local disk is ~94 % full
    82	  (≈228 GB free): check `df -h /home` before any large write; keep big arrays on the cluster.
    83	- Memory: large quadrature tables OOM (Gauss 112³ at R′=1024 needed 42.9 GiB; the 4096² R512 2D bank
    84	  alone is ≈64 GiB). Memory pilot first, using actual device bytes (143,771 MiB on H200).
    85	- Retrains are not bitwise reproducible: record seeds and hashes.
    86	
    87	## 6. GPU-hour ledger
    88	Keep `experiments/<lane>/runs/GPU-LEDGER.json`: every job's id, GPU type, measured elapsed GPU-hours
    89	(from `sacct`), and cumulative total. Submit a new job only if `spent + certified projection ≤ cap`.
    90	Projections come from a smoke-derived cost certificate (pattern: `worktrees/2026-10-14-jcp-speed3d/
    91	experiments/jcp-speed3d/resources.py`). Never exceed your cap; defer items in your lane's defer order
    92	and record them.
    93	
    94	## 7. Lessons (each cost a lane before)
    95	- Every background wait has an **overall deadline**, a `timeout` on each remote call, and checks that
    96	  the awaited **process is alive** (`ps -p <pid>`), not only that a file is missing. Never write an
    97	  `until`/`sleep` loop without a deadline. One agent waited 18 h on a Codex file that was never written.
    98	- Every Codex call has `timeout` and `< /dev/null`; check the output file afterwards.
    99	- Acceptance checkers recompute every predicate from raw outputs. Forgery-only findings are "noted",
   100	  not blocking.
   101	- Analysis and scoring on CPU.
   102	- If you hit a usage limit and are resumed, re-read your HEARTBEAT and last commits before acting.
   103	
   104	## 8. Heartbeat (mandatory)
   105	Write `<worktree>/experiments/<lane>/HEARTBEAT.json` at least every 20 minutes while working, and
   106	immediately before and after every long wait (job queue, Codex audit, local run). Keep it uncommitted
   107	or commit it — either is fine — but keep it current. Schema:
   108	```json
   109	{"lane": "...", "utc": "2026-10-17T23:40:00Z", "step": "6: codex job audit for p1c",
   110	 "waiting_on": {"slurm_jobs": ["5301xxx"], "codex_output": "audits/codex-job-p1c-1.md",
   111	                "codex_pid": 12345, "local_pid": null},
   112	 "expected_finish_utc": "2026-10-18T01:00:00Z", "last_commit": "abc1234",
   113	 "gpu_hours_spent": 3.2, "gpu_hours_cap": 20, "notes": "..."}
   114	```
   115	Use `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`. Read-only Codex watchdogs check these files every ~10 minutes; a
   116	heartbeat older than 60 minutes with no live wait gets you resumed by the coordinator.
   117	
   118	## 9. Writing
   119	Only inside your own worktree. Never write to main, `LAB-LOG.md`, `notebook/`, another worktree, or
   120	another lane's cluster namespace. Commit small and often; after every commit run your lane's
   121	`sync_github.sh` (never `git push` an exp/ branch). Files > 50 MB: SHA256 manifest. Reports: level-1
   122	title, status line, LaTeX maths, mermaid diagrams, provisional labels inline, closing plain-language
   123	glossary, every number generated. Never mention or include the heat POD-128 comparison. Never quote a
   124	pre-September Burgers speed-up. Retractions matter more than successes: if something contradicts an
   125	earlier report, say so plainly.
   126	
   127	## 10. Hand-back (final message to the coordinator)
   128	- what ran (job ids, GPUs, namespace, GPU-hours from the ledger);
   129	- the key numbers, read from the generated report, with each hypothesis verdict (pass/fail/inconclusive);
   130	- what is provisional and why;
   131	- anything wrong or retracted;
   132	- every audit file with its verdict;
   133	- paths of report, plots (PNG), DESIGN, ledger;
   134	- the last lane commit and its mirror commit;
   135	- deferred items and open questions.
   136	
   137	## 11. Disk hygiene for Codex audits (added 2026-10-18 02:50 UTC by the coordinator)
   138	Codex audits have been copying ~10 GB of lane data into `/tmp/<lane>-roundN-audit/` per round (speed3d:
   139	89 GB in rounds 5–12, local disk fell to 95 GB free). From now on: every Codex prompt must say "do not copy
   140	large arrays; if you need scratch, use at most 2 GB under `/tmp/<lane>-audit-scratch/` and reuse it";
   141	after each audit round the lane agent deletes that round's scratch dir once the audit file is read
   142	(check `pgrep -af <dir>` first). Check `df -h /home` before each audit; do not start one below 60 GB free.
   143	
   144	## 12. Waiting on a process (added 2026-10-18 03:10 UTC)
   145	Never wait with `pgrep -f <pattern>` where the pattern also appears in your own waiter's command line — it
   146	matches itself and never ends (happened in jcp-p1-scaling). Wait on the **PID** (`while kill -0 $PID`)
   147	captured when you launched the process, or run the Codex call in the foreground with `timeout`.

