    43	Every accuracy number previously reported against the old references can move, case by case, by at most the "old vs new" distance of that case (same arm fields, same lattice, fixed-$t_0$ normalisation; A1.9); the per-cohort maxima are in the old-versus-new section. The new references carry the uncertainty $\epsilon_{ref}$ listed with each case: two arms are rankable on a case only if their errors differ by more than $2\epsilon_{ref}$ (an estimated resolution limit, conditional on the asymptotic regime the order check tests).
    44	
    45	## Method
    46	
    47	Viscous Burgers on $(0,1)^d$ with zero Dirichlet walls, outputs at $t_k=0.05k$, $k=0..5$ (the campaign PDEs, cohorts and ICs, unchanged):
    48	
    49	$$ u_t + u\sum_{a=1}^{d}\partial_a u = \nu\,\Delta u . $$
    50	
    51	$R_{ST}$: central differences in space, Crank–Nicolson in time with a Rannacher start (the first two steps replaced by four backward-Euler half steps), three nested levels with $\Delta t\propto h$ — 2D $L=2048, 4096, 8192$ intervals, 3D $L=128,256,512$ — refined to 2D $L=12288$ / 3D $L=768$ when a case fails. $R_S$: the same space discretisation with backward Euler at the reduced model's $\Delta t_0=0.005$. Per case and output time, with $d_{12}=\lVert u_1-u_2\rVert$, $d_{23}=\lVert u_2-u_3\rVert$ on the scoring lattice and $n_0=\lVert u(t_0)\rVert$:
    52	
    53	$$ \frac{d_{12}}{d_{23}}=\frac{h_1^{p}-h_2^{p}}{h_2^{p}-h_3^{p}},\qquad \epsilon_{ref}=\max_{k\ge1}\frac{d_{23}(t_k)}{n_0}\,\frac{h_3^{p}}{h_2^{p}-h_3^{p}} . $$
    54	
    55	A case is accepted iff every step met the Newton rule, $p\in[1.8,2.2]$ at every $t_k$, the cell Péclet number is below 2, both level differences exceed ten times the solver-residual floor proxy, the shared IC matches, and the error vectors are direction-consistent ($c\le0.5$). The cross-check is an independent sine pseudo-spectral solver with ETDRK4 time stepping on 8 validation cases per dimension; it must agree with the finest level within $2\epsilon_{ref}$ while its own self-difference is below $\epsilon_{ref}/2$.
